WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.680
In a medieval battle, mistaking friend for foe&nbsp;
could have deadly consequences. This became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.920
clear on a foggy morning in April 1471 near&nbsp;
Barnet, north of London, when the Houses of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.680
York and Lancaster clashed in battle. Right at the&nbsp;
start of the fighting, the Earl of Oxford managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.120
to drive the Yorkists on the left flank from the&nbsp;
battlefield. However, when he returned, the allied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:32.320
archers mistook his badge—a shining star—for&nbsp;
their enemy Edward IV’s Sun in Splendour and began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:40.560
shooting at their own men. The result was utter&nbsp;
chaos, dozens of deaths, and ultimately defeat.&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.800
This incident from the Wars of the Roses&nbsp;
illustrates a fundamental difficulty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.480
medieval warfare: without modern uniforms amid&nbsp;
the din of battle, it was often difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.960
know who was standing in front of you. So how&nbsp;
did people tell which side a soldier was on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.960
Medieval armies used different identifying&nbsp;
marks, depending on the time and place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.040
to distinguish individual fighters, groups within&nbsp;
an army, and entire armies in the heat of battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:14.240
In this video, we’ll take a look at them.
How Medieval Armies Told Friend from Foe&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.760
First and foremost, it’s important to realize that&nbsp;
a medieval battle was not a chaotic free-for-all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:26.240
in which the two sides mingled and men lashed out&nbsp;
wildly in all directions. Instead, it consisted of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:32.560
a series of coordinated attacks and counterattacks&nbsp;
between closed formations, such as shield walls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:39.200
It was therefore vitally important to stay compact&nbsp;
and keep the ranks closed. When two formations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.880
clashed, it was therefore immediately obvious who&nbsp;
belonged to which side—the one standing beside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.360
you was your friend; the one standing in front&nbsp;
of you and trying to stab you was your enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.880
Nevertheless, chaotic situations arose time&nbsp;
and again—for example, during cavalry charges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.920
the arrival of reinforcements, or when the&nbsp;
formation did break up. In those cases, other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.680
identifying marks were needed.
[Small Group Cohesion]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:16.080
The simplest and oldest way to distinguish a&nbsp;
friend from an enemy was by looking at his face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.080
In the Middle Ages, a soldier did not simply fight&nbsp;
somewhere in a crowd of armed men; rather, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.120
part of a group—for example, a knight’s retinue,&nbsp;
a local militia, or a company of mercenaries. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.200
a result, he almost always fought side by side&nbsp;
with men he knew well—such as vassals, friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.800
and relatives. Just how close these bonds were&nbsp;
is illustrated by the example of the Swiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:48.400
soldiers whom Charles the Bold of Burgundy had&nbsp;
executed after the siege of Grandson in 1476. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.920
chronicler Petermann Etterlin writes: “[H]ere&nbsp;
hanged a father and a son next to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.080
there two brothers or other friends. And&nbsp;
there came the honorable men who knew them;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.280
who were their friends, cousins and&nbsp;
brothers, who found them miserably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:15.200
hanging.” This description could just as easily&nbsp;
have applied to the Anglo-Saxons on Senlac Hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.600
the French at Bouvines, or any other medieval&nbsp;
army. They were all composed of smaller groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.480
whose members were closely related and knew each&nbsp;
other’s names and faces. And it was much harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:37.280
to mistake a fellow soldier you knew for an enemy.
[Banners and Standards] Because of this structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.520
of medieval armies, the most important point of&nbsp;
reference on the battlefield was the group—and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:51.040
identifying mark was the flag, first as a&nbsp;
banner, later as a standard. As a symbol of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.320
shared identity, it held a unit together,&nbsp;
served as a rallying point and signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:02.080
and enabled the soldiers to coordinate their&nbsp;
movements and maneuvers. When the banner advanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:08.800
the soldiers advanced as well; when it turned,&nbsp;
the soldiers turned as well. Without the banner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.920
there was no organized unit, but only an&nbsp;
armed mob. This strict visual and physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:22.560
grouping made it clear who belonged to which&nbsp;
unit—and thus who was friend and who was foe.&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.440
The army as a whole also carried a banner, which&nbsp;
was usually held near the rear, or close to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.840
army’s leader. In an emergency, this was the point&nbsp;
where the troops gathered for a last stand. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:41.520
Emperor Frederick II raided the camp of the&nbsp;
Lombard League at the Battle of Cortenuova,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.640
the surprised Lombards retreated to the&nbsp;
banner on their Carroccio , regrouped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:53.120
and thus withstood the imperial attacks until&nbsp;
they were able to withdraw during the night.&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.920
Precisely because the banner embodied the&nbsp;
order and honor of a unit, it was a key target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:06.560
Its fall could shatter a unit’s morale and&nbsp;
often signaled the end of organized combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.960
At the Battle of Hattin, Saladin even told his&nbsp;
men that victory would not be complete until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:20.560
red banner of the King of Jerusalem had fallen.
[Battle Cries] Another highly intuitive tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.760
that played an important role throughout the&nbsp;
Middle Ages and beyond was the battle cry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.440
These cries demonstrated the army’s unity&nbsp;
and helped soldiers identify friend from foe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.280
especially when the chaos of battle, dust, or&nbsp;
darkness limited visibility. The Crusaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:43.920
for example, often shouted “Deus Vult,” the&nbsp;
French “Montjoie!” and the English “St. George.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.600
In doing so, they bolstered their courage,&nbsp;
called upon a powerful saint for protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.960
and loudly proclaimed their unity. Armies&nbsp;
also frequently had specific battle cries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.480
that they used as a password to identify their&nbsp;
own men in moments of uncertainty—for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:07.920
when someone approached the camp in the dark.
[Shields] Another important identifying mark was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.600
the shield. For much of the Middle Ages, it was&nbsp;
the most important defensive weapon and, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.680
same time, provided a highly visible surface.&nbsp;
As early as the early Middle Ages, warriors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.440
painted their shields with colors and patterns to&nbsp;
distinguish themselves from other fighters and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.800
show which group they belonged to. This practice&nbsp;
was particularly widespread among the Franks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.480
Saxons, and Normans. The Bayeux Tapestry, which&nbsp;
depicts Norman knights carrying shields painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.280
in various designs, illustrates just how&nbsp;
diverse these shields could be. Such simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:49.120
symbols would later evolve into coats of arms.
[Heraldry and Crests] In the High Middle Ages, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:55.200
nature of warfare changed. Knights increasingly&nbsp;
wore closed helmets that concealed their faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:02.240
and armies became larger and more impersonal. In&nbsp;
the 12th and 13th centuries, heraldry gave rise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.640
two new ways of identifying individual combatants:&nbsp;
coats of arms displayed on shields and surcoats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:14.960
and ornaments worn on helmets, known as crests.&nbsp;
Over time, the simple symbols we have just seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.280
on the horsemen’s shields in the Bayeux Tapestry&nbsp;
evolved into fixed identifying marks passed down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.720
through generations—the coats of arms by&nbsp;
which a man could be clearly recognized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.400
or at least identified with a particular&nbsp;
family or lord. Coats of arms were first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:39.360
worn only on the shield, and later also on&nbsp;
tunics, lance pennants, and horse blankets.&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:46.000
The second new identifying feature was a striking&nbsp;
ornament on the helmet—the so-called crest—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.920
was also a heraldic element. A knight could&nbsp;
be recognized by it from a great distance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.520
The crest usually consisted of a wooden&nbsp;
or wire frame covered with paper-mâché,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.960
leather, or fabric and displayed the same&nbsp;
motif as the coat of arms on the shield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:08.320
In the second half of the 14th century, these&nbsp;
crest ornaments became increasingly elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:14.240
and were imaginatively decorated with additional&nbsp;
elements. To keep track of all these symbols and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:20.400
determine which knight was which—and, above&nbsp;
all, on which side he was fighting—specialists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:28.000
in heraldry, known as heralds, were employed.
In the 14th and 15th centuries, as plate armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.440
became widespread and gradually made the shield&nbsp;
obsolete, people began to decorate the armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.200
itself—if they could afford it. The gauntlets&nbsp;
of the Black Prince, Edward of Woodstock, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:45.760
example, had knuckle guards adorned with tiny lion&nbsp;
figures, the symbol of the English kings , and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:53.360
Charles the Bold wore a suit of armor emblazoned&nbsp;
with the Cross of St. Andrew of Burgundy.&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.560
[Ribbons, Symbols, Uniforms] However, coats&nbsp;
of arms, crest ornaments, and ceremonial armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.160
were reserved for the military elite. For the&nbsp;
rank-and-file foot soldiers, the focus remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:10.720
on symbols that identified an entire group, such&nbsp;
as colors, ribbons, or badges. These symbols were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:17.760
often very simple and occasionally even determined&nbsp;
on the spot. A color, a sewn-on cross, or even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:24.160
twig attached to a helmet or clothing sufficed as&nbsp;
a distinguishing mark. At the Battle of Andernach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:30.560
in 876, for example, Louis the Younger had his&nbsp;
men wear white cloth ribbons to distinguish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.080
them from the troops of Charles the Bald.
In the late Middle Ages, such common symbols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:41.120
became more standardized. During the Hundred&nbsp;
Years’ War, English and French troops began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:47.280
to use standardized insignia. The English mostly&nbsp;
used the red Cross of St. George, while the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:54.080
often wore a white cross. The soldiers of Burgundy&nbsp;
were identified by a red St. Andrew’s cross, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:00.560
can be seen in this image of the Battle of Murten.
Another option was for a lord or a city to outfit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:06.800
its troops with clothing in a uniform color; for&nbsp;
example, the city of Tournai equipped its men with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:13.520
red tunics. In the 15th century, this evolved&nbsp;
into a special form surcoat known as a livery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.200
Liveries were particularly widespread in England&nbsp;
during the Wars of the Roses. In addition to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:25.200
and provisions, a lord provided his retinue with&nbsp;
these short tabards in his colors, often bearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:31.360
his personal insignia, such as the shining star&nbsp;
of the Earl of Oxford in the introductory example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:37.360
This looked much like a uniform, but it merely&nbsp;
indicated which lord’s retinue a man belonged to;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.520
equipment and clothing remained individual.
Medieval soldiers did not need uniforms to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.880
distinguish friend from foe. They relied on a&nbsp;
complex, ever-changing system of identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:55.920
marks—and it worked so reliably that mix-ups&nbsp;
like the one at Barnet remained the exception.

